<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- The host application is sandboxed, may talk to smart cards, and
     may make outbound connections.

     The client grant exists for one feature: an archival signature
     (PAdES-B-LTA) must fetch a qualified timestamp and the revocation
     data proving its chain, or it is not archival. What travels is a
     digest and a certificate identifier - never a key, a PIN, or one
     byte of the document.

     There is no network-server grant: the MVP ships with the SCS
     (the loopback signing interface, FEATURE_SCS) off, so nothing
     binds a socket. Restore com.apple.security.network.server here
     when that feature ships - see Config/Features.xcconfig. -->
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.smartcard</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The open and save panels. A sandboxed app reaches a file the
	     holder picked only through Powerbox, and Powerbox declines to
	     show a panel at all without this - which looks from the outside
	     like a button that does nothing.

	     It grants nothing by itself: access follows what the holder
	     chose in the panel, one file at a time, and there is no
	     bookmark kept afterwards. Dropping a document already grants
	     that document; this is what lets the signed one be written. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- There is no application-group grant: the MVP ships with
	     contactless card reading (FEATURE_CONTACTLESS) off, and the
	     group container is only the channel that carries a contactless
	     card's access number to the token driver. Without the grant
	     macOS creates no group container at all. Restore it, on this
	     app and the token extension, when that feature ships - see
	     Config/Features.xcconfig. -->
</dict>
</plist>
